WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.320
- What's next for you?

00:00:01.380 --> 00:00:03.330
- Just kinda try to keep a hold on the job
in center

00:00:04.019 --> 00:00:06.300
and be a leader for the guys on defense.

00:00:12.020 --> 00:00:12.890
- As a rookie, you're

00:00:12.890 --> 00:00:13.580
just trying to survive.

00:00:14.180 --> 00:00:14.930
You know, your first,

00:00:14.930 --> 00:00:15.970
your first part of the season,

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:18.470
it's like, “I want to prove I belong here.”

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:21.259
- Don't feel like you’ve gotta go out and

00:00:21.259 --> 00:00:23.960
prove anything to anybody. Because

00:00:23.960 --> 00:00:25.370
when you feel like you gotta start

00:00:25.370 --> 00:00:27.110
trying to prove stuff to people or show

00:00:27.110 --> 00:00:29.270
people, that's when stuff starts going

00:00:29.270 --> 00:00:31.400
the wrong way. My first time up, it's,

00:00:31.400 --> 00:00:32.450
you know, you're trying to get the

00:00:32.450 --> 00:00:34.220
nerves out and all that kinda stuff.

00:00:34.790 --> 00:00:36.019
My first time up didn't

00:00:36.019 --> 00:00:37.400
really go too well.

00:00:38.390 --> 00:00:39.739
That's when I realized that the

00:00:39.739 --> 00:00:42.560
grass at Wrigley Field will eat up

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.120
any kind of ground ball. I don't know

00:00:44.120 --> 00:00:45.500
if it's still doing it right now,

00:00:45.980 --> 00:00:46.550
Kyle.

00:00:46.755 --> 00:00:48.885
But if you get a chance, man, don't

00:00:48.885 --> 00:00:50.085
hit the ball on the ground at Wrigley,

00:00:50.085 --> 00:00:52.035
bro, cuz it will eat it up.

00:00:53.265 --> 00:00:54.465
- Doc, Devin, you guys have talked about this.

00:00:54.465 --> 00:00:56.565
So, as pitchers, you don't get

00:00:56.565 --> 00:00:58.665
to hide. I make an error,

00:00:58.875 --> 00:01:01.515
I get a chance to hit. You guys

00:01:01.515 --> 00:01:03.285
give up a big hit in a situation,

00:01:04.095 --> 00:01:05.325
manager pulls you out.

00:01:05.474 --> 00:01:07.035
Fans are letting you have it. Cameras on

00:01:07.035 --> 00:01:08.055
you all the way to the dugout. You

00:01:08.055 --> 00:01:09.045
don't get a chance to hide.

00:01:09.285 --> 00:01:10.935
So, when you lose that confidence—and

00:01:10.935 --> 00:01:13.515
I'll start with you, Doc—that’s inside of

00:01:13.515 --> 00:01:15.765
you, where do you go to get that back?

00:01:15.765 --> 00:01:16.605
What do you do?

00:01:16.695 --> 00:01:17.955
Or how do you get out of your own way?

00:01:18.515 --> 00:01:22.815
- Aw, this is tough. I remember my first big league start.
I was in Houston, and my parents was there.
- [Announcer:] There are the parents.

00:01:23.115 --> 00:01:24.915
They came over from Florida to watch

00:01:24.945 --> 00:01:26.835
Dwight Gooden in his big league debut.

00:01:28.295 --> 00:01:29.525
- After the game, my dad said,

00:01:29.525 --> 00:01:30.455
“Son, what do you think?”
- I said, “Well,

00:01:30.455 --> 00:01:32.164
I should win a lot of games.”

00:01:32.555 --> 00:01:34.475
My next start was at Wrigley Field.

00:01:34.475 --> 00:01:35.664
I got knocked out in the second inning.

00:01:35.675 --> 00:01:38.815
I gave up, like, 800 runs. My dad
called. He said, “Son, what do you

00:01:39.535 --> 00:01:41.645
think now?” I said, “Well, I don't know if I’m ready.”
First time you go through something

00:01:41.645 --> 00:01:43.115
like that, it's a wakeup call.

00:01:43.655 --> 00:01:45.215
Once you get out there, just hittin’

00:01:45.225 --> 00:01:46.985
the first thing like, “Yo, gave up a hit too early.”

00:01:47.375 --> 00:01:48.664
Then I started thinking, “Oh, man.

00:01:48.730 --> 00:01:49.420
Here we go again.”

00:01:49.930 --> 00:01:51.400
If I started thinking that way, then,

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:52.600
all right, here we go again.

00:01:53.060 --> 00:01:54.910
But I said, “OK, let me just make quality
pitches.

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:56.500
Don’t try and make a perfect pitch.

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:57.670
Just make a quality pitch.”

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.130
And at that point, if I had a bad game

00:02:00.130 --> 00:02:01.870
or whatever, I had to understand that

00:02:02.590 --> 00:02:06.430
the fans, if they happened to boo me or write
something bad, it’s about my performance, not

00:02:06.520 --> 00:02:09.530
necessarily me because they didn’t know me.
And I was able to separate the two.

00:02:10.090 --> 00:02:12.220
Then I was OK. But it helped me in

00:02:12.220 --> 00:02:13.750
the long run. You know, in, like, Year

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:15.730
2, Year 3, I was able to say, “OK,

00:02:15.730 --> 00:02:16.600
I've been there before.

00:02:16.780 --> 00:02:17.800
Let me just put that aside,

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:18.310
and let me get back

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:18.630
to what I do best.”

00:02:19.540 --> 00:02:21.820
And go from there.
- Devin, let's

00:02:21.820 --> 00:02:23.590
be honest: Guys get jealous.

00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:25.540
You're having this success, and, “OK,

00:02:25.540 --> 00:02:26.530
young fella, it's not gonna always

00:02:26.530 --> 00:02:27.730
be this easy.” And guess what?

00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:28.120
They're right.

00:02:28.420 --> 00:02:29.679
How were you accepted in the

00:02:29.679 --> 00:02:31.410
clubhouse?
- I mean, we have a great

00:02:31.410 --> 00:02:32.929
group of guys, so it was, it was

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:34.290
nothing but love from everyone.

00:02:34.589 --> 00:02:36.179
And, I mean, it was just a good support

00:02:36.179 --> 00:02:37.709
system for me to have, especially being

00:02:37.709 --> 00:02:39.989
a rookie, you know? There was times where

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:41.489
I didn't feel like I was throwing that

00:02:41.489 --> 00:02:44.370
well, and there was a week or two that

00:02:44.609 --> 00:02:46.649
I just didn't feel like me on the mound.

00:02:46.649 --> 00:02:47.790
And, like, everyone.

00:02:48.240 --> 00:02:49.680
that I talked to just, you know, they

00:02:49.680 --> 00:02:51.420
were super encouraging cuz they all

00:02:51.420 --> 00:02:53.840
wanted to see me do well also.
- And then comes

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.390
Kyle Lewis. I enjoy watching you play, what

00:02:56.390 --> 00:02:57.800
you do, the way you go about your game.

00:02:58.220 --> 00:03:00.590
And you’re a game-changer. Going forward,

00:03:01.430 --> 00:03:02.720
won the Rookie of the Year, that's enough

00:03:02.720 --> 00:03:04.670
pressure. But what's next for you?

00:03:04.980 --> 00:03:06.620
- Just kinda try to keep a hold on

00:03:06.620 --> 00:03:08.450
the job in center and be a leader

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:11.090
for the guys on defense. And, you

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.170
know, then at the plate, just

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:13.519
trying to have a simple approach.

00:03:14.020 --> 00:03:14.890
Trust what I can do.

00:03:15.010 --> 00:03:16.000
Bring some confidence to the

00:03:16.000 --> 00:03:17.860
game and bring some confidence to the team

00:03:17.890 --> 00:03:18.670
when I come up to the plate.

00:03:18.970 --> 00:03:20.260
I try to embody that and try to,

00:03:20.650 --> 00:03:21.910
try to be a leader in that way.

00:03:22.120 --> 00:03:22.950
- I'm glad you said

00:03:22.950 --> 00:03:25.650
that because, going forward,

00:03:25.950 --> 00:03:27.420
that's what you're gonna have to do.

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:29.070
Day after day, people are gonna

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:31.079
look up to you. As a rookie with

00:03:31.079 --> 00:03:32.609
this energy, you won this award.

00:03:32.820 --> 00:03:34.350
Now, you're one of the leaders

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:36.570
on the team. Grow on that.

00:03:37.980 --> 00:03:38.730
That's not pressure.

00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:39.840
That's something you embrace.

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.190
I saw Ryan embrace it.

00:03:41.190 --> 00:03:43.200
He inspired me. Understand that

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:44.730
you will inspire your teammates,

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:46.049
guys that have been around trying

00:03:46.049 --> 00:03:47.160
to figure out how to get through

00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:48.660
it. To see you have that success,

00:03:48.930 --> 00:03:51.000
you will continue to inspire

00:03:51.000 --> 00:03:52.560
them to be better themselves.

00:03:52.700 --> 00:03:53.780
- Like he said, man, just

00:03:53.780 --> 00:03:55.519
continue to inspire each other,

00:03:55.519 --> 00:03:56.239
your teammates.

00:03:56.730 --> 00:03:57.810
Just do what you do.

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.730
Like, don't feel like you gotta go

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:02.280
above and beyond. Just do what God gave

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:03.420
you, and you're gonna be all right.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:04.770
And great things will happen, man.

00:04:05.340 --> 00:04:05.580
Great things

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:06.120
will happen.

